# Every environment variable kobai reads. Copy to `.env` — which is gitignored — and edit.
# `docker compose up` works with the defaults below; nothing here is a secret worth keeping.
#
# **In a git worktree you may find `.env` already here.** A worktree gets a copy of this file
# written for it the first time you run anything, with POSTGRES_PORT and PORT filled in from
# a hash of its path, so sixteen of them do not collide on one port (ADR-0084). It is written
# once and never over: from then on the file is yours.

# --- The reference Project (reference/src/server.ts) -------------------------------------

# Postgres connection string. Core applies its migrations against this at boot and refuses
# to start if they fail. Leave it unset for `pnpm run dev`, which runs the Project on the
# host against `pnpm run db`: it assembles this address from POSTGRES_USER, POSTGRES_PASSWORD,
# POSTGRES_DB and POSTGRES_PORT below, at the moment it needs it, so the container and the
# Project cannot end up disagreeing. Set it to point somewhere else — a real database, a
# colleague's — and that wins. `pnpm run up` never reads this; the container talks to
# `db:5432` inside the compose network.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://kobai:kobai@localhost:55432/kobai

# Port the application listens on — the API and the Admin, which is at /admin-ui on the same
# origin (ADR-0010, ADR-0033). `pnpm run admin:dev` reads it too, because that is where its
# dev server proxies kobai's surfaces to; the browser still sees only the dev server's origin.
# 3000 unless this is a git worktree, in which case a port in 53000-53999 was derived from
# its path and written on the line below — the same way POSTGRES_PORT is, and sharing its last
# three digits, so 53154 beside 55154 reads as one checkout. `pnpm run up` prints the URL it
# is on either way. Set it to pin your own.
# PORT=3000

# Who this deployment's FIRST Merchant is. Both are read at boot, and the Merchant is created
# then — kobai has no unauthenticated write path, so there is no way to create one over HTTP
# on a deployment that holds none: nobody would hold the permission it needs (ADR-0041).
#
# Set both or neither; `compose.yaml` forwards them to the app container, so `pnpm run up`
# reads them from here. Booting with neither leaves the deployment with no Merchant and says
# so in its log; booting with one of the two says which half is missing. Booting again
# changes nothing — a deployment that already has a Merchant is left exactly as it is found,
# so these can be removed once the first boot has done its work, and rotating them here does
# not create a second account.
#
# The password is deliberately blank rather than filled in. A working default here would be a
# credential every deployment that did not read this line shared — which is the failure this
# whole mechanism replaced, wearing a different hat. Left blank, the boot says which half is
# missing and creates nothing. Twelve characters minimum; length is what matters.
#
# It is stored as an argon2id digest and is never recoverable, but it exists in whatever set
# it — this file, a compose file, a shell history — so treat it as a secret. kobai never
# prints it. Changing it afterwards is not built yet, and is tracked separately.
KOBAI_INITIAL_MERCHANT_EMAIL=owner@example.test
KOBAI_INITIAL_MERCHANT_PASSWORD=

# What this checkout takes payments a Shopper completes at their bank with (ADR-0070), and the
# **secret** store API key its own routes at `/payments/…` place the resulting Orders with —
# never the publishable key a storefront ships to a browser (ADR-0055).
#
# **All four blank is the ordinary checkout, and is what `pnpm run ci` must always be**: this
# Project then settles out of band through `reference/src/payments/manual.ts`, mounts no payment
# routes, and serves everything else exactly as before. The gate has no Stripe secret and must
# never acquire one, so nothing here has a value and `vitest.config.ts` blanks all four for the
# suite whatever a shell exports.
#
# Filling in the three Stripe ones — all three, or the boot says which are missing and settles
# out of band anyway — is what makes this Store take cards, FPX and GrabPay through
# `@kobai/plugin-stripe`. `reference/.env.example` says what each one is; a real key belongs in
# `.env`, which is gitignored, and nowhere else.
# KOBAI_STORE_API_KEY=
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# STRIPE_PAYMENT_PAGE_URL=

# --- compose.yaml ------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The database the `db` service comes up holding, and the one everything else here signs in
# to. Change any of the three and both addresses below are rebuilt from the new value,
# percent-encoded, so the container and the suite cannot end up disagreeing about the
# password (ADR-0084, carrying ADR-0046's rule). That used to be exactly what happened:
# compose read this file, the harness did not, and the only symptom was an authentication
# failure naming neither.
#
# A value with spaces or punctuation is fine, and needs double quotes if it holds a `#` or a
# trailing space; `export` in front of it is read the same either way. Four things it may not
# hold, none of which this file can fix:
#   - `$` anywhere — docker compose interpolates variables inside a quoted value here, so
#     `pa${POSTGRES_USER}word` reaches the container as `pakobaiword` while Node's own reader
#     passes it through untouched, which is the disagreement all of this exists to prevent.
#   - a trailing newline — the shell drops one before the address is built.
#   - `/`, `?` or `#` in POSTGRES_DB — a database name travels in the path of a URL, and the
#     `pg` driver decodes that part with `decodeURI`, which leaves a reserved character's
#     escape exactly as it found it. The user and password have no such limit.
#   - `/`, `?` or `#` in POSTGRES_PASSWORD *if you also run `pnpm run up`* — the app
#     container's DATABASE_URL is assembled by compose, which has no way to encode anything.
#     The suite is unaffected; `scripts/env.ts` encodes the addresses it builds.
# Point KOBAI_TEST_DATABASE_URL and DATABASE_URL somewhere by hand if you need past those.
POSTGRES_USER=kobai
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=kobai
POSTGRES_DB=kobai
# Host port for the `db` service. Deliberately not 5432 — a Developer's own Postgres should
# not have to move. 55432 unless this is a git worktree, in which case a port in 55000-55999
# was derived from its path and written on the line below (AGENTS.md § The ports belong to
# the checkout). Set it to pin one — the value then decides the test harness's port too, so
# the two cannot disagree.
# POSTGRES_PORT=55432

# Which compose project this checkout's containers and volume belong to. Left to compose,
# which names it after this directory — worktree directories have different names, so their
# containers and volumes are already separate. Set it to pin one.
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=kobai

# --- Tests ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The Postgres the test harness connects to. It creates and drops a throwaway database per
# test file, so this points at the maintenance database rather than at a test database.
# Leave it unset: `vitest.config.ts` builds it from the port and the credentials above, which
# is the whole point — one set of values decides what the container comes up as and what the
# suite dials. Set it and it wins over all of them, and percent-encoding it is then yours.
# KOBAI_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://kobai:kobai@localhost:55432/kobai
